  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.amc-10a-2006-p10.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "Let \\(k = \\sqrt{120 - \\sqrt{x}}.\\) Since \\(\\sqrt{x} \\ge 0,\\) we need \\(0 \\le k \\le \\sqrt{120},\\) so \\(k \\in \\{0, 1, \\ldots, 10\\},\\) giving \\(11\\) values.\n\nEach \\(k\\) yields \\(\\sqrt{x} = 120 - k^2,\\) and since \\(120 - k^2\\) is positive and strictly decreasing, the resulting values \\(x = (120 - k^2)^2\\) are distinct.\n\nThus, the correct answer is E.",
      "hints": [
        "Let \\(k = \\sqrt{120 - \\sqrt{x}}\\) and bound \\(k\\)",
        "\\(k\\) is an integer with \\(0 \\le k \\le 10\\)"
      ],
      "images": []
    }
